      "side": 1,
      "message": "We can make this a little safer and less error prone by using \"span consumption\". Here\u0027s an example generated by Gemini. Double-check that it\u0027s correct:\n\n\n----\n\n### The Rationale: Why Span Consumption?\n\nWhen working with raw memory buffers—especially in firmware tooling where an out-of-bounds read/write can cause a segfault or brick a device—safety and readability are paramount.\n\nThe traditional C/C++ approach uses a `for` loop, an index variable (`i` or `offset`), and manual arithmetic to track boundaries. The **span consumption pattern** flips this: instead of moving an index through a static view of memory, you continuously shrink the view itself until there is nothing left to process.\n\nHere is why this is a structural improvement:\n\n1. **Eradicates Index Arithmetic:** You remove the `offset` variable and the `buff.size() - offset` calculation. This is the most common source of off-by-one errors in C++.  \n2. **Self-Documenting Intent:** Methods like `.first(n)` (give me the next `n` bytes) and `.subspan(n)` (discard the first `n` bytes and give me the rest) clearly express what the code is doing to the memory, rather than hiding the intent behind pointer math.  \n3. **Implicit Bounds Safety:** By driving the loop with `!remaining.empty()`, it is structurally impossible to loop past the end of the buffer.\n\n---\n\n### The Changes Needed\n\nHere is how to apply this pattern to the three buffer-processing functions in `iap_control.cc`.\n\n#### 1\\. Refactoring `WriteRomData`\n\n**Before:**\n\n```c\nstd::expected\u003cvoid, IapError\u003e IapControl::WriteRomData(\n    std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e buff) {\n  if (buff.empty()) return std::unexpected(IapError::BufferEmpty);\n\n  for (size_t offset \u003d 0; offset \u003c buff.size(); offset +\u003d kEp3Length) {\n    size_t chunk_size \u003d std::min(kEp3Length, buff.size() - offset);\n    auto chunk \u003d buff.subspan(offset, chunk_size);\n    // ... validation and callback\n  }\n  return {};\n}\n\n```\n\n**After:**\n\n```c\nstd::expected\u003cvoid, IapError\u003e IapControl::WriteRomData(\n    std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e buff) {\n  if (buff.empty()) return std::unexpected(IapError::BufferEmpty);\n\n  const size_t total_size \u003d buff.size();\n  auto remaining \u003d buff; // Create a mutable view of the const data\n\n  while (!remaining.empty()) {\n    size_t chunk_size \u003d std::min(remaining.size(), kEp3Length);\n    \n    // Slice off the front chunk, then advance the remaining view\n    auto chunk \u003d remaining.first(chunk_size);\n    remaining \u003d remaining.subspan(chunk_size); \n\n    if (auto res \u003d IapSendData(chunk); !res) {\n      LogErr(\"[IAP] {} - fail\\n\", __func__);\n      return std::unexpected(res.error());\n    }\n\n    if (progress_callback_) {\n      progress_callback_(total_size - remaining.size(), total_size);\n    }\n  }\n  return {};\n}\n\n```\n\n#### 2\\. Refactoring `ReadRomData`\n\nThis function mirrors the write operation. The only difference is it operates on a mutable `std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e` and uses `kEp4Length`.\n\n**After:**\n\n```c\nstd::expected\u003cvoid, IapError\u003e IapControl::ReadRomData(std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e buff) {\n  if (buff.empty()) return std::unexpected(IapError::BufferEmpty);\n\n  const size_t total_size \u003d buff.size();\n  auto remaining \u003d buff;\n\n  while (!remaining.empty()) {\n    size_t chunk_size \u003d std::min(remaining.size(), kEp4Length);\n    \n    auto chunk \u003d remaining.first(chunk_size);\n    remaining \u003d remaining.subspan(chunk_size);\n\n    if (auto res \u003d IapRecvData(chunk); !res) {\n      LogErr(\"[IAP] {} - fail\\n\", __func__);\n      return std::unexpected(res.error());\n    }\n\n    if (progress_callback_) {\n      progress_callback_(total_size - remaining.size(), total_size);\n    }\n  }\n  return {};\n}\n\n```\n\n#### 3\\. Refactoring `GetChecksum`\n\nBecause `GetChecksum` validates that the buffer is 4-byte aligned at the very beginning, we don\u0027t even need `std::min` to protect the boundaries. We know we can safely consume exactly 4 bytes per iteration until the span is empty.\n\n**Before:**\n\n```c\nstd::expected\u003cuint32_t, IapError\u003e IapControl::GetChecksum(\n    std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e buff) {\n  if ((buff.size() % 4) !\u003d 0) return std::unexpected(IapError::BufferTooSmall);\n\n  uint32_t checksum \u003d 0;\n  for (size_t i \u003d 0; i \u003c buff.size(); i +\u003d 4) {\n    if (auto val \u003d Get32Le(buff.subspan(i, 4))) {\n      checksum +\u003d *val;\n    }\n  }\n  return checksum;\n}\n\n```\n\n**After:**\n\n```c\nstd::expected\u003cuint32_t, IapError\u003e IapControl::GetChecksum(\n    std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e buff) {\n  if ((buff.size() % 4) !\u003d 0) return std::unexpected(IapError::BufferTooSmall);\n\n  uint32_t checksum \u003d 0;\n  auto remaining \u003d buff;\n\n  while (!remaining.empty()) {\n    // Consume exactly 4 bytes, then advance\n    if (auto val \u003d Get32Le(remaining.first(4))) {\n      checksum +\u003d *val;\n    }\n    remaining \u003d remaining.subspan(4);\n  }\n  \n  return checksum;\n}\n\n```",
